1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,856
In 2024, more than five thousand Americans
went to work and never came home.

2
00:00:04,896 --> 00:00:08,581
But the risk is not spread evenly.

3
00:00:08,621 --> 00:00:12,509
In some jobs, it's more than thirty times
higher than average.

4
00:00:13,009 --> 00:00:16,169
Here are the ten most dangerous jobs in
America,

5
00:00:16,209 --> 00:00:18,321
ranked by real death rates from the U.S.

6
00:00:18,361 --> 00:00:21,513
Bureau of Labor Statistics.

7
00:00:21,553 --> 00:00:22,606
For the average worker,

8
00:00:22,646 --> 00:00:26,413
the rate is three point three deaths per
hundred thousand.

9
00:00:26,453 --> 00:00:27,741
Keep that number in mind.

10
00:00:28,241 --> 00:00:29,310
Number ten.

11
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:31,221
Grounds maintenance workers.

12
00:00:31,261 --> 00:00:36,693
That's landscapers, gardeners, and the
people who climb and cut trees.

13
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:40,054
Their rate: about twenty-one deaths per
hundred thousand.

14
00:00:40,554 --> 00:00:42,807
The danger isn't the lawn mower.

15
00:00:42,847 --> 00:00:43,562
It's the tree.

16
00:00:43,602 --> 00:00:46,892
Climbers fall, cut branches swing the
wrong way,

17
00:00:46,932 --> 00:00:50,393
and a chainsaw high up leaves no room for
mistakes.

18
00:00:50,893 --> 00:00:52,046
Number nine.

19
00:00:52,086 --> 00:00:53,713
The truck driver.

20
00:00:53,753 --> 00:00:57,022
The rate is about twenty-six per hundred
thousand.

21
00:00:57,062 --> 00:01:00,134
But this job has something no other job on
this list has.

22
00:01:00,634 --> 00:01:06,280
More truck drivers die at work than people
in any other single job.

23
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:10,491
Almost eight hundred heavy truck drivers
in 2024.

24
00:01:10,531 --> 00:01:14,295
Long hours, tired drivers, and every day
on the highway.

25
00:01:14,795 --> 00:01:15,949
Number eight.

26
00:01:15,989 --> 00:01:18,822
The underground mining machine operator.

27
00:01:18,862 --> 00:01:23,402
They drive the huge machines that cut coal
and rock deep below the surface.

28
00:01:23,902 --> 00:01:27,320
Their rate: about thirty-six per hundred
thousand.

29
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:31,564
Underground, the dangers are heavy
machines in tight tunnels, falling rock,

30
00:01:31,605 --> 00:01:32,982
and very few ways out.

31
00:01:33,482 --> 00:01:34,480
Number seven.

32
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:36,598
The construction helper.

33
00:01:36,638 --> 00:01:41,070
They carry, lift, hold and clean up for
the skilled trades.

34
00:01:41,110 --> 00:01:43,714
Often it's someone's first job on a
building site.

35
00:01:44,214 --> 00:01:46,370
And that's part of the problem.

36
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:50,787
New workers are still learning where the
danger is.

37
00:01:50,827 --> 00:01:54,151
Their rate is almost thirty-six per
hundred thousand,

38
00:01:54,191 --> 00:01:56,045
more than ten times the average.

39
00:01:56,545 --> 00:01:57,555
Number six.

40
00:01:57,595 --> 00:01:58,813
The pilot.

41
00:01:58,853 --> 00:01:59,769
This one surprises people,

42
00:01:59,809 --> 00:02:04,393
because flying on an airliner is one of
the safest ways to travel.

43
00:02:04,893 --> 00:02:09,438
Most of the risk is in small planes: crop
dusters, bush pilots,

44
00:02:09,478 --> 00:02:15,079
air taxis and small cargo flights, often
in bad weather and remote places.

45
00:02:15,119 --> 00:02:19,018
The rate for pilots: about thirty-seven
per hundred thousand.

46
00:02:19,518 --> 00:02:20,320
Number five.

47
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:21,207
The garbage collector.

48
00:02:21,247 --> 00:02:26,714
You see them every week, and probably
never think of it as dangerous.

49
00:02:27,214 --> 00:02:30,477
But their rate is about thirty-seven per
hundred thousand.

50
00:02:30,517 --> 00:02:35,257
They work on the road, early in the
morning, often in the dark,

51
00:02:35,297 --> 00:02:39,473
right next to passing traffic and their
own heavy truck.

52
00:02:39,973 --> 00:02:41,150
Number four.

53
00:02:41,190 --> 00:02:42,756
The ironworker.

54
00:02:42,796 --> 00:02:46,978
They build the steel skeletons of bridges
and skyscrapers,

55
00:02:47,018 --> 00:02:49,696
walking on narrow beams high above the
ground.

56
00:02:50,196 --> 00:02:54,701
Their rate: about thirty-eight per hundred
thousand.

57
00:02:54,741 --> 00:02:59,798
They wear harnesses, but one slip, one
gust of wind, one loose beam,

58
00:02:59,838 --> 00:03:00,964
can still be fatal.

59
00:03:01,464 --> 00:03:02,168
Number three.

60
00:03:02,208 --> 00:03:03,498
The roofer.

61
00:03:03,538 --> 00:03:08,923
Every house needs a roof, and someone has
to stand on it, on a slope,

62
00:03:08,963 --> 00:03:11,875
in the sun or the wind, carrying heavy
bundles.

63
00:03:12,375 --> 00:03:16,940
The rate jumps here: almost forty-nine per
hundred thousand.

64
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:20,283
That's about fifteen times the average
job.

65
00:03:20,323 --> 00:03:22,370
And the main killer is simple.

66
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:23,232
Falls.

67
00:03:23,732 --> 00:03:24,430
Number two.

68
00:03:24,470 --> 00:03:26,423
The commercial fisherman.

69
00:03:26,463 --> 00:03:29,339
Out at sea, far from help, in cold water,

70
00:03:29,379 --> 00:03:32,312
with heavy gear and a deck that never
stops moving.

71
00:03:32,812 --> 00:03:36,503
Their rate is about eighty-nine per
hundred thousand.

72
00:03:36,543 --> 00:03:40,337
When something goes wrong out there, a
boat can sink fast,

73
00:03:40,377 --> 00:03:42,830
and the nearest rescue can be hours away.

74
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:45,037
Number one.

75
00:03:45,077 --> 00:03:45,011
The logger.

76
00:03:45,051 --> 00:03:48,712
They cut down huge trees in steep forests,

77
00:03:48,752 --> 00:03:51,152
often far from any road or hospital.

78
00:03:51,652 --> 00:03:55,970
Their rate: about one hundred ten deaths
per hundred thousand.

79
00:03:56,010 --> 00:04:00,040
That's more than thirty times the average
worker.

80
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:04,048
And most of those deaths come from the
same thing: a falling tree,

81
00:04:04,088 --> 00:04:06,482
or a log that rolls the wrong way.

82
00:04:06,982 --> 00:04:08,450
Think about that.

83
00:04:08,490 --> 00:04:12,518
A job where the most dangerous moment is
the very thing you came to do.

84
00:04:13,018 --> 00:04:16,363
So what do these jobs have in common?

85
00:04:16,403 --> 00:04:16,330
Heights.

86
00:04:16,370 --> 00:04:17,471
Heavy machines.

87
00:04:17,511 --> 00:04:18,633
Moving vehicles.

88
00:04:18,673 --> 00:04:19,352
Water.

89
00:04:19,392 --> 00:04:20,640
Falling trees.

90
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:23,000
And very often, working far from help.

91
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:25,989
And notice what is not on the list.

92
00:04:26,029 --> 00:04:27,419
Firefighters face real danger,

93
00:04:27,459 --> 00:04:32,855
but their death rate is about four per
hundred thousand.

94
00:04:32,895 --> 00:04:37,385
Police and other protective jobs come in
around eight and a half.

95
00:04:37,425 --> 00:04:40,580
The deadliest jobs are often the ones we
barely notice.

96
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:43,211
The good news: safety rules,

97
00:04:43,251 --> 00:04:47,113
harnesses and better equipment have saved
many lives.

98
00:04:47,153 --> 00:04:51,445
But next time you see someone on a roof,
on a garbage truck, or up a tree,

99
00:04:51,485 --> 00:04:52,636
remember.

100
00:04:52,676 --> 00:04:55,054
Their workday is a lot riskier than yours.

101
00:04:55,554 --> 00:04:57,939
Which of these jobs would you never do?

102
00:04:57,979 --> 00:04:59,480
Tell us in the comments.

103
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:02,259
And if you liked this, subscribe.

104
00:05:02,299 --> 00:05:06,133
Next time: the jobs that might disappear
in the next ten years.

